{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>For this commandment, etc. — it is not too wondrous for you.</b> And afterward it does not say 'from you' but says simply: 'and it is not far off.' There is room here to explain this passage in two ways — whether regarding the commandments of the Torah in general, or whether regarding the repentance that was mentioned above." "<b>Way one — regarding all the commandments of the Torah.</b> For in every commandment there are two aspects: one is the performance itself, to do the commandment in actual deed; the second is the intention of the commandment, to understand and discern the secrets of the Torah which YHWH concealed from the nations, as it says (Psalm 25:14): ״סוֹד ה׳ לִירֵאָיו״, etc., and it is written (ibid. 147:20): ״לֹא עָשָׂה כֵן לְכָל גּוֹי וּמִשְׁפָּטִים בַּל יְדָעוּם״ — for YHWH concealed from the nations the secrets of the Torah and delivered them to Israel. Therefore He said: 'it is not too wondrous for you' — for if it should arise upon your heart to say: How shall I perform the commandment without intention, like a donkey carrying books? — of this He said: the matter is not so, but rather 'it is not too wondrous for you.' For wondrous is the deed of YHWH from the nations, but not from you, as it says (ibid. 139:14): ״נִפְלָאִים מַעֲשֶׂיךָ וְנַפְשִׁי יֹדַעַת מְאֹד״ — for the tablets are the work of Elohim and are truly wondrous from the nations, but 'my soul knows exceedingly well,' for You have heard my prayer, ״אַל תַּסְתֵּר מִמֶּנִּי מִצְוֹתֶיךָ״ (ibid. 119:19) — spoken regarding the secrets of the Torah. And regarding the actual deed He said: 'and it is not far off' — not from you alone, but from all the nations it is not far, for YHWH did not command you to bring from afar any commandment. For regarding the offerings, our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Leviticus Rabbah 27:6) that the Holy One, blessed be He, said: I did not burden you to bring an offering from species that are not in your possession, but only from cattle and sheep that are in your possession, etc. And from this you may draw an analogy to all the commandments — that the Holy One, blessed be He, did not command you to bring from afar anything for a commandment, but only in things that are readily available to you." "And he returned to explain matter by matter, for what I said — 'it is not too wondrous for you' — that is the wisdom given from heaven, for 'YHWH gives wisdom, from His mouth knowledge and understanding' (Proverbs 2:6). And so that you should not say that the actual performance of the commandments YHWH gave to the earth, but not the wisdom — for it is still in heaven — of this He said: 'it is not in heaven,' etc. For both the deed and also the contemplation and the path of understanding — all of it YHWH gave to the earth. And what I said — 'and it is not far off' — of this He said: 'and it is not beyond the sea,' for I did not command you to bring anything from beyond the sea for a commandment. 'For the word is very near to you, in your mouth and in your heart, to do it' — 'in your heart' is the place of contemplation and wisdom, and 'in your mouth' is the place of performance, for even at a time when no commandment comes to hand, it is within your power to do it with your mouth, as our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Menahot 110a): whoever engages in the study of the law of the burnt-offering is as though he offered it, etc. And this analogy applies to all other commandments — that whoever engages in them is as though he performed them. This is what is said: 'in your mouth and in your heart, to do it' — for you can repay bulls with your lips and it is considered as though you did them. And likewise even if one thought in his heart to perform a commandment and was compelled and did not do it, the Holy One, blessed be He, accounts it to him as though he did it (Berakhot 6a) — this is what is said: 'in your mouth and in your heart, to do it.'" "The second way is that the statement 'for this commandment' speaks of the repentance that was mentioned in the passage above. And regarding it He said: 'it is not too wondrous for you' — for you have no excuse to say that you did not know that the Holy One, blessed be He, accepts the repentance of sinners, for you know the matter of repentance more than all the nations, for Israel and repentance preceded the world (Pesahim 54a), and if so, when YHWH created repentance, you were there as well. And if the nations need urging regarding repentance, like the people of Nineveh — behold, you do not need urging regarding it, for the secret of repentance is revealed to you from of old and from earliest times, and 'it is not too wondrous for you.' For 'it is not in heaven, to say, who will go up for us to heaven and take it for us' — to say that YHWH did not demand of us trampling His courts, a man of sin and much rebellion, and who would go up to heaven to ask the mouth of YHWH whether He will accept us to Him in repentance — for this secret is already revealed to you. Or it stands on what was said above, 'and you shall return unto YHWH your Elohim.' And if you should say: if my sins caused this — that the Shekhinah withdrew from the lower realms and ascended on high — if so, I return to Him but He will not return to me, for I caused this distance, and how shall I return unto YHWH? Must I not then ascend to heaven and return to Him, blessed be He? And if so, it is a wonder in my eyes: where shall I go toward His spirit, blessed be He? Of this He said: 'it is not too wondrous for you' — even from you, the matter of returning unto YHWH is not wondrous, and you do not need to ascend to heaven to Him, blessed be He. For as soon as you have prepared your heart to return to Him, blessed be He, then He, blessed be He, turns and returns to you, to bring His Shekhinah, blessed be He, down upon you." "<b>And it is not far off.</b> Since our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Yoma 86b): What constitutes a penitent? Such as one in whom that same deed comes to hand and he withdraws from it. Rav Judah pointed: in that same chapter and in that same place and with that same woman. And if so, one who sinned with a woman beyond the sea — perhaps you would say he has no remedy until he crosses the paths of the seas to that very place where he sinned, the very passage through which he transgressed — and through this he might hold back from repentance. Of this He said: the matter is not so. 'For the word is very near to you, in your mouth' — confession; 'and in your heart' — remorse; and then this suffices for you. For your mouth and your heart are the place of the sin — for the heart bent you and caused you to deviate, and it is the place of repair through repentance — which is to say, also in that same place. For the requirement of that same woman was stated only for the freer rendering of the matter, but the essence of repentance is not dependent on this." "And some say that He spoke concerning repentance: for from the side of the exaltedness of El, blessed be He, repentance is a wondrous thing, beyond wonder — for any man who divorces his wife will not return to take her after she has been defiled, so how would a King, exalted and uplifted, draw near to Himself men who sin in their very beings, who have turned away from following Him and gone astray after foreign gods? But 'from you' — that is, from your side — 'it is not too wondrous,' in the manner of what is said (Psalms 103:14): 'זָכוּר כִּי עָפָר אֲנָחְנוּ' — for from the side of matter, there is no righteous man on earth who does good and does not sin (Ecclesiastes 7:20); therefore it is fitting that He accept your repentance. And perhaps it is concerning this that He said: 'לֹא בַשָּׁמַיִם הִיא' — for it is not found among the host of the height of the spiritual beings, but rather in you, O man born of dust; therefore from your side repentance is a fitting thing and acceptable in the manner that has been explained."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice 